<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ix Chel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ixchel</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ix Chel is a Maya goddess associated with the moon, childbirth, weaving and medicine. In the Postclassic period pilgrims from across Yucatán, especially women seeking fertility and safe childbirth, travelled to her shrines on the island of Cozumel to consult her oracle. Scholars link her with the goddesses shown in the surviving Maya codices, though the exact identification is debated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ix-chel.jpg" alt="Ix Chel" width="130" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ixchel_Dresden.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ixchel is the 16th-century name of the aged jaguar goddess of midwifery and medicine in Maya culture. In the 1500s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Diego de Landa</a> called her the goddess of making children and also of medicine, and described a feast, Ihcil Ixchel, held in the month of Zip by physicians and shamans, who brought out divination stones and medicine bundles with little idols of her.</p><p>She was already known to the Classic Maya. Karl Taube showed that she corresponds to Goddess O of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden-codex">Dresden Codex</a>, an aged woman with jaguar ears, whose codical name is now generally read Chak Chel. A key piece of evidence is the Birth Vase, a Classic container showing a childbirth presided over by old women headed by an old jaguar goddess, all with weaving implements in their headdresses. On another Classic vase Goddess O acts as a physician.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ixchel has several aspects. An entwined serpent forms her headdress, crossed bones may decorate her skirt, and she sometimes has claws instead of hands and feet, features shared with Aztec earth goddesses such as Toci and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cihuacoatl">Cihuacoatl</a>, who were invoked by midwives. As a jaguar goddess she could also be pictured as a fearsome woman warrior with shield and spear. In the Madrid Codex she is assimilated to a rain deity, with rain pouring from her armpits and abdomen, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden-codex">Dresden Codex</a> includes her in almanacs of the rain gods, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaac">Chaacs</a>, usually inverting a water jar.</p><p>The only myth about her preserved is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verapaz">Verapaz</a> tale related by Las Casas, in which she and her spouse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzamna">Itzamna</a> had thirteen sons, two of whom created heaven and earth. Her mythology may once have centred on the sweatbath, where Maya mothers went before and after giving birth, as the Aztec Toci was goddess of the sweatbath.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In the early 16th century Maya women seeking a fruitful marriage travelled to the sanctuary of Ix Chel on the island of Cozumel, the most important place of pilgrimage after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichen-itza">Chichen Itza</a>, where a priest hidden in a large statue gave oracles. To the north Hernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> named a smaller island the Island of Women for the idols of goddesses he found there, among them Ixchel. The head town of the Chontal province of Acalan, Itzamkanac, venerated her as one of its main deities, and one of its coastal settlements was called Tixchel, at the place of Ixchel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ixchel” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shrines drew pilgrims from afar, such as Cholula, the sacred city of Quetzalcoatl with the largest pyramid in the world by volume, and the island of Cozumel, where women came to the goddess of childbirth, <b>Ix Chel</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mesoamerican-religion">Mesoamerican religion · Community &amp; Society</a></li><li><blockquote>The League of Mayapan then held the northern lords together until the 1440s, and pilgrims crossed to the shrine of the goddess <b>Ix Chel</b> on the island of Cozumel.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maya-civilisation">Maya civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Pilgrims came from across Yucatán to consult the oracle of <b>Ix Chel</b>, goddess of childbirth and the moon.</blockquote><a href="/state/cozumel">Cozumel</a></li></ul></section>
